I've read the phase's report, findings, the scanner it publishes, every test file it changed, and the production code those tests exercise. Here is what I found.

```
FINDINGS:
1. [MAJOR] phases/phase-088/scan.py:247 — the phase's central evidence is 334 lines of new code with no test of its own, and its assertion test is unsound in the direction that matters: `asserts_something` returns True on the bare *mention* of `take_output` / `.output` / `stdout` / `expect_err` (`READS_THE_PROGRAM.search(code)`, scan.py:82-84, 249) and on any `assert_eq!` in the body (`COMPARES_A_VALUE`, scan.py:87, 251), with no check that the value read is compared to anything. A test that reads the program's output and never asserts on it is therefore scored as non-vacuous — and reading `take_output()` is exactly the idiom this phase introduced in every new test. The scanner's "vacuous tests: 0" (REPORT.md:185-187, 157-160) is consequently weaker than the report presents it, and nothing in the repository would notice the scanner drifting: `scan.py` is not invoked by any test, and the only thing that depends on it is a number in the report. The repo already has the precedent for testing a phase artefact (`tests/report_markdown_test.rs` pins phase-084's two documents); this phase's larger artefact has no equivalent. Concrete fix: add a test that runs the scanner against a fixture directory holding one test that calls `take_output()` without asserting and one that asserts, and asserts the scanner names exactly the first; and tighten `asserts_something` to require that what was read is compared. I could not run the scanner (the reviewer is read-only), so I could not measure how many of the 69 files' tests are affected — VERIFIED by tracing scan.py:249 `if READS_THE_PROGRAM.search(code): return True`, scan.py:251 `if COMPARES_A_VALUE.search(code): return True`, and scan.py:269-270 `functions = find_functions(code)` / `bodies = {name: ... for ... in functions}`, which reads only the file being scanned.
2. [MINOR] phases/phase-088/scan.py:15 — the docstring says the `tests/common/` helpers "are read through the tests that call them", but the code reads them nowhere: `bodies` (scan.py:270) is built from `find_functions(code)` of the file being scanned, and `helper_reach` returns `("", "")` for any name not in that same-file map (scan.py:274). `tests/common/vm.rs`, `tests/common/corpus.rs` and `tests/common/shrink.rs` are therefore never read, and `tests/differential_test.rs` — the only file that uses them — keeps its assertions in helpers the scanner cannot see. REPORT.md:200 claims "The scanner's own docstring says the same thing, in step with the code it runs"; this is the one place where it does not. Concrete fix: change the sentence to say the `tests/common/` helpers are not followed, or follow them.
3. [MINOR] tests/redblue_test.rs:14 — the module doc asserts "The program is run **once**", but `edge_the_value_a_program_ends_on_is_a_number_and_not_its_rendering` runs its fixture twice: `lines_of(source)` at tests/redblue_test.rs:128 and `run_isolated(&program)` at tests/redblue_test.rs:133, then again at :146 and :150. The doc names that exact trap as the thing this file had to stop doing ("calling it as well would be a second execution of the same fixture"), and FINDINGS.md:30-36 makes the same claim for the phase. The two assertions do not cross-contaminate — each reads its own run — so no assertion is wrong; what is wrong is a claim of "once" in the file whose whole subject is the honesty of its claims. Concrete fix: read the lines and the value from the one `Vm` (the value is already what `run_isolated` returns), or say "once per claim".
4. [STYLE] tests/unreviewed_work_ack_test.rs:249 — `DELIVERY_REASON` enumerates the files the delivery commit carries ("the phase's own suites (tests/stdlib_module_docs_test.rs, tests/loop_bounds_test.rs, tests/redblue_test.rs and tests/text_split_join_test.rs ...)"), and the enumeration is now short: this round added `tests/failure_channel_test.rs`, and the same diff also carries `tests/loop_control_test.rs`, `tests/time_builtin_test.rs` and `phases/phase-088/scan.py`. This string is the acknowledgement the audit records against the pipeline's own commit, so a reader checking the commit against its reason finds the reason incomplete. Concrete fix: add the missing files to the sentence.
5. [STYLE] tests/failure_channel_test.rs:28 — the three programs `sqrt("nine")`, `abs("three")` and `text.split(1, ",")` are already pinned by `tests/stdlib_module_docs_test.rs:431`, which asserts the same three through `redblue::run_source` (a `Runtime` error naming the builtin) *and* against each engine separately. The new test re-runs the same programs through the same entry point, so the only genuinely new coverage in the file is the index row, the unknown-function row and the parse-error row. Concrete fix: narrow the new file to those three, or fold the three builtins into the existing test.
```

What I checked and found **sound**, so it is not in the list: all 17 reachability-only tests named by the scanner really do now assert the program's answer (I read each one); the 13 new `#[test]` functions reconcile with `1967 + 13 = 1980`; the per-file numstat sums to the gate's quoted `+2077 -70`; the nine `examples/`+`modules/` files the gate block names all exist; the phase's `REPORT.md`/`FINDINGS.md` fences are balanced with no info-string fence inside an open fence, so the `## What changed` / `## Tests added` / `## Gates` / `## Known gaps` headings are visible outside code blocks; `Expr::Expect` does answer `Err(Error::Runtime(..))` at src/interpreter.rs:2331, so the two `catch`-based tests' "the run coming back `Ok` proves the catch fired" argument holds; a loop statement really is worth `Value::Nothing` (src/interpreter.rs:1914) and `repeat "five" times` really runs zero turns (src/value.rs:213), so the two loop tests assert what they say; `stdlib::builtin` is the single resolution point for both engines (src/stdlib.rs:759, src/bytecode/vm.rs:2759), so the "both engines refuse identically" assertions are well-founded; and the index refusal really does say "out of bounds" (src/runtime.rs:123).

